A||||8||kidzwheelz||Experimental Fiero Found???||||5||
Z||000000||kidzwheelz||02-23-2000||04:14 PM||||  Went to a dealer near my house who has about five Fieros sitting around. (All in different stages of ill-repair) I spotted a Convertible Fiero and thought it might make an interesting project. Guy walks out, tells me "That car is the only one on my lot thats not for sale." I ask why, of course, to which he replys "One of twelve experimentals, got it documented from Pontiac and everything." Anyway, I looked it over, left him my card, and told him to call me if he changed his mind about selling it. Now my question, Have any of you ever seen a factory experimental fall into private hands and then get documented by the manufacturer?<BR>Does anyone besides me think this guy is full of ......?<p>[This message has been edited by kidzwheelz (edited 02-23-2000).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||william_thorniii||02-23-2000||04:43 PM||||kidz,<P>The only thing that I have heard of that is remotely similar went like this.  GM donates test mules to various technical and vocational schools.  I heard of a Toledo area that had an 88 GT with a factory 200 bhp 6 and power steering.  Unfortunately, the cars that are donated for educational use cannot be titled and must be totaled out at the end of their educational life.  This particular car was suffered severe body damage.  Many panels and some parts of the frame were cut with a water-jet cutter in order to total it.  When it arrived at the bone yard, two individuals stumbled across it and somehow managed to purchase it.  I have never seen this car and therefore cannot certify its authenticity.  I did talk with the co-owner (financial backer) at length.  I even asked (or begged) him to allow me to talk with the other owner (the car-nut) and get to see it.  I was willing to drive the 4 hours to get there, but the guy was in fact a moron (Although an engineer moron) and would not divulge the location of the vehicle or contact information.  Of course this is all speculation based on 1 idiot.  He did seem to be an honest idiot, but an idiot none the less. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000002||HF6582||02-23-2000||04:52 PM||||Don't know how GM is, but back in 93 Ford let loose a Mustang with the 4.6DOHC to a Texas dealership.  <P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000003||SCCA FIERO||02-23-2000||08:19 PM||||kidzwheelz, if it's a maroon 87 coupe... he's full of BS.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||02-23-2000||09:13 PM||||There's a slight chance that he's not full of BS.  According to Witzenberg, Pontiac did build at least three convertibles:<P>1) A gold 84 roadster with a chopped windshield and no top whatsoever.  In mid-84 GT bodywork was added and the car hit the show circuit.<BR>2) An 84 (photo is B/W - car looks white or silver) with a folding hardtop.  Ugly but clever.<BR>2) A red 86 GT with red leather interior, a folding cloth top, Centerline wheels and a side-opening clamshell rear deck.  Bad-ass.<P>If it's not one of these three cars, the guy is probably wrong about it being a factory convertible.  There were a few aftermarket convertibles sold new through dealers - maybe it's one of those.  There's some info on those on the Michigan Fiero Club's website (sorry, I don't know the URL).||||reg||1
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||02-24-2000||12:52 AM||||kidzwheelz and I live in the same town and there is an 87 flip-top coupe running around. I have talked to a guy that used to own it and checked it over.<P>BTW... that red 86GT Fiero with the Centerline wheels... I have those same wheels for my Fiero.||||reg||1
